{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Any man, etc., who slaughters.</b> The reason for the doubling ״אִישׁ אִישׁ. In Zevaḥim (108a) they expounded according to Rabbi Shimon to include one who slaughters for a layman, who is liable, as if it said ״אִישׁ לְאִישׁ.״ And they did not expound it to include two who slaughtered, because the verse says ״לָאִישׁ הַהוּא וְנִכְרַת הָאִישׁ הַהוּא״ — and they expounded the word ״הַהוּא״ once to exclude the mistaken, the coerced, and the unwitting, and once to exclude two who slaughtered in this manner — ״הַהוּא״ and not them. But it is difficult, for in Torat Kohanim they expounded to exclude two who slaughtered from the written word ״דָּם שָׁפָךְ,״ and not from the word ״הַהוּא.״ And one may say that since there are two exclusions — ״הַהוּא״ and ״שָׁפָךְ״ — and in Torat Kohanim they expounded one exclusion for the unwitting, the coerced, and the mistaken, and one exclusion to exclude two who slaughtered, the Gemara is not particular about finding the exclusion of two who slaughtered from ״שָׁפָךְ״ or from ״הַהוּא.״ See also the verse that follows this one." "<b>Who slaughters.</b> In Torat Kohanim they said, and this is their language: ״I have only… whence for slaughtering a bird? Scripture says ״אוֹ אֲשֶׁר יִשְׁחַט.״״ — ״But is it not a logical inference? Just as slaughter… and so too melikah… the inference does not hold, therefore Scripture says ״אֲשֶׁר יִשְׁחַט״ — he is liable for the slaughter but is not liable for the melikah,״ end quote. This implies that from the word ״אוֹ״ he derives slaughter of a bird, and from ״אֲשֶׁר יִשְׁחַט״ he excludes melikah, which would otherwise have been derived by an a fortiori argument. But it is difficult: the baraita that he brought in tractate Zevaḥim (107a) demonstrates that Rabbi Yishmael derives from ״אֲשֶׁר יִשְׁחַט״ to include slaughter for a bird, and from the verse's saying ״זֶה הַדָּבָר״ he excludes melikah, which would otherwise have been derived by an a fortiori argument. And also according to Rabbi Akiva, who disagrees there with Rabbi Yishmael, he derives the inclusion of bird slaughter from ״דָּם יֵחָשֵׁב״ and excludes melikah from ״אֲשֶׁר יִשְׁחַט,״ as is demonstrated from the Gemara there. If so, in the baraita of Torat Kohanim it is neither Rabbi Akiva nor Rabbi Yishmael. And one must perforce say that the baraita of Torat Kohanim does not follow Rabbi Yishmael, since it expounds there ״זֶה הַדָּבָר״ as a gezerah shavah: just as ״זֶה הַדָּבָר״ and so forth — ״אֶל רָאשֵׁי הַמַּטּוֹת״ and so forth; if so, it must perforce not follow Rabbi Yishmael. Also, from the fact that in Torat Kohanim he did not expound the extra word ״דָּם יֵחָשֵׁב״ for the derivation of Rabbi Yishmael to include one who dashes the blood, it is thus demonstrated that the one who expounds it follows Rabbi Akiva. Also, from the fact that they expounded in parashah 10 ״אוֹ זָבַח״ to include the blood — according to the view of Rabbi Akiva in the baraita that he brought in the Gemara of Zevaḥim — this points with a finger that the exposition of the Tanna of Torat Kohanim follows the path of Rabbi Akiva. From this point forward you would derive bird slaughter from ״דָּם יֵחָשֵׁב״ and the exclusion of melikah from ״אֲשֶׁר יִשְׁחַט״ — so why does the Tanna of Torat Kohanim derive both expositions from ״אוֹ אֲשֶׁר יִשְׁחַט״? Furthermore, for the Tanna of Torat Kohanim who derives both expositions from ״אוֹ אֲשֶׁר יִשְׁחַט,״ why do I need the ״דָּם יֵחָשֵׁב״ that the verse states?" "<b>And I saw</b> in the Rambam, of blessed memory, who wrote in chapter 18 of Hilkhot Ma'aseh HaKorbanot, and this is his language: ״Two who grasped the knife and slaughtered are exempt, as it is said ״אֲשֶׁר יִשְׁחַט״ ״אוֹ אֲשֶׁר יִשְׁחַט״ — one and not two,״ end quote. And it is difficult regarding the Rambam, of blessed memory, why he fashioned a new derivation and did not record the derivation that was mentioned in the baraita and in the Talmud. And it is further difficult regarding the Rambam, of blessed memory: behold, the verse ״אֲשֶׁר יִשְׁחַט״ ״אֲשֶׁר יִשְׁחַט״ — they expounded it for the law of the bird, whether according to the Tanna of Torat Kohanim, whether according to Rabbi Yishmael, whether according to Rabbi Akiva, whose words were brought in Zevaḥim (107a) as we wrote above. ״אֲשֶׁר יִשְׁחַט״ is needed to teach about the bird — according to one, to include liability for its slaughter, and according to another, to exclude melikah. And this law the Rambam ruled at the end of chapter 19 of Hilkhot Ma'aseh HaKorbanot. And it is further difficult with what the Rambam wrote — the liability for one who slaughters for a layman — from the verse ״דָּם יֵחָשֵׁב לָאִישׁ הַהוּא דָּם שָׁפָךְ,״ as he wrote, and this is his language: ״Even though he did not intend to slaughter these consecrated animals for HaShem, he is nonetheless liable, as it is said ״דָּם יֵחָשֵׁב… דָּם שָׁפָךְ״ — even though this blood is in his thought like spilled blood, not like a sacrifice, he is nonetheless liable,״ end quote. But behold, the Gemara derives the liability for one who slaughters for a layman from His saying ״אִישׁ אִישׁ,״ and furthermore ״דָּם שָׁפָךְ״ they expounded in Torat Kohanim to exclude two who slaughtered, or to exclude the coerced, the unwitting, and the mistaken, as we wrote above — so what place is there to derive from it one who slaughters for a layman? And behold, this exposition they stated in Zevaḥim (108b) according to Rabbi Yosei, who disagrees with Rabbi Shimon in the exposition of ״אִישׁ אִישׁ.״" "And for this one may say that the Rambam chose to bring the proof from the extra word ״דָּם שָׁפָךְ,״ which is a stronger derivation than the exposition of ״אִישׁ אִישׁ,״ which is not a strong derivation. And the proof is from what they said in tractate Menaḥot (Bava Metzia 31a) regarding the verse ״הַעֲבֵט תַּעֲבִיטֶנּוּ,״ that Rabbi Shimon holds ״the Torah spoke in the language of human beings,״ and the Tosafot wrote there that in a place where there is some contradictory proof to the exposition that is heard from the doubling of words, we expound the doubled word and we do not say ״the Torah spoke in the language of human beings,״ and when there is opposition to the exposition of the doubling we say ״the Torah spoke in the language of human beings.״ From this point forward, the exposition of the doubling ״אִישׁ אִישׁ״ is not as strong as the exposition of ״דָּם שָׁפָךְ״ that the Gemara brought according to Rabbi Yosei. And even though the Gemara said it only according to Rabbi Yosei, nevertheless from it we may take for Rabbi Shimon, for there is no necessity to make a dispute between them on this matter. And even though the Gemara stated according to Rabbi Shimon's view that he derives one who slaughters for a layman from ״אִישׁ אִישׁ״ (Zevaḥim 108a), that was before he knew the exposition of ״דָּם יֵחָשֵׁב… דָּם שָׁפָךְ״ — whereas after this exposition it is a matter equal for all of them, and therefore the Rambam wrote from the exposition of ״דָּם שָׁפָךְ.״ But our first difficulty regarding the Rambam's words remains in its place." "<b>And it appears</b> to say that the Rambam holds as follows: since we see so many variations — both between the baraita of Torat Kohanim and the baraita that the Gemara brought in Zevaḥim, and between the words of the Gemara itself, as we wrote above — if so, the matter demonstrates that one should not be particular about the change of locus for these derivations. And therefore, regarding what the Rambam proves from the verse ״דָּם שָׁפָךְ״ that one who slaughters for a layman is liable — if so, we no longer have a source from which to derive the exclusion of two. For this reason he sought the derivation of the extra word ״אֲשֶׁר יִשְׁחַט,״ which is superfluous, and we derive bird slaughter only from the word ״אוֹ,״ as they expounded in Torat Kohanim — that it derives slaughter for a bird from the word ״אוֹ״ as an inclusion. And according to this, we derive the exclusion of melikah in a bird from His saying ״זֶה הַדָּבָר״ to exclude melikah in a bird, according to the view of Rabbi Yishmael as we wrote above, who derives the exclusion of bird melikah from ״זֶה הַדָּבָר.״ And it should not be difficult for you from what we hold (Eruvin 46b) that the halakhah follows Rabbi Akiva over his colleague, for there is nothing here contrary to Rabbi Akiva regarding the matter of halakhah."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {0 "The abbreviation ש״ס is expanded as HaShas (the Talmud) throughout.", 2 "The reference קח: is normalized as 108b for the edition cited; the baraita of Torat Kohanim is the Sifra.", 3 "The citation מציעא with page reference ב״מ לא. is normalized to Bava Metzia 31a."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:58:18.209728038Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}